## FILE5_41 ordinary prefix read: one raw read

For the ordinary non-pipe path in `magic.c:500-514`, FILE5_41 issues one raw `read(fd, buf, bytes_max)` call. The pure-Go implementation now preserves that boundary for Linux real `*os.File` values by using `SyscallConn.RawControl` plus one `syscall.Read`. Custom `io.Reader` values and non-Linux builds retain the existing `Reader.Read` path.

The syscall result normalizes a negative byte count to zero before slicing the buffer. A `RawControl` failure is reported as `EBADF`, matching the closed-file error class required by the focused comparison. No retry loop, synthetic bytes, classification, or production entry-point change was introduced.

Focused ptrace SETREGS faults at offsets 0, 4, 11, and 5 matched native FILE5_41 and Go API behavior for short and long samples. The strict 772-path gate remained 100% equal across description, MIME, dispatch signature, auxiliary checks, and error text. Production remains cgo while the remaining I/H/J/K/L work is open.


## FILE5_41 default magic path and C-string path boundaries

The FILE5_41 `magic_getpath` behavior used by `magic_load(NULL)` is now represented in the compatibility adapter: an explicit `MAGIC` environment value wins; on Unix the loader checks `$HOME/.magic.mgc`, then `$HOME/.magic` or `$HOME/.magic/magic.mgc`, and appends the compiled system path `/etc/magic` as the native fallback chain. Windows keeps a small explicit fallback (`magic.mgc`) and is not claimed as native Windows default-path parity.

File names and compiled database paths now stop at the first NUL before reaching Go filesystem calls, matching the C-string boundary used by `magic_file`, `magic_load`, and the compatibility wrapper. `Open("")` uses the default-path route; a non-empty Go string that becomes empty only after NUL truncation remains an explicit empty path and does not silently become the default route. The `.mime` compatibility lookup, source-directory parsing, and remaining loader errors are still open.
